 ## Overview
-Network architectures in NeRF define the function $F_\Theta: (\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{d}) \rightarrow (\sigma, \mathbf{c})$ that maps 3D spatial positions $\mathbf{x}$ and viewing directions $\mathbf{d}$ to volume density $\sigma$ and view-dependent color $\mathbf{c}$ [G]. While the standard NeRF architecture uses two MLPs, variants modify this structure to handle dynamic scenes, noise, specific object manipulations, or efficiency constraints. These architectures differ from siblings like [Multi-input MLP (mi-MLP)](16599-multi-input-mlp-mi-mlp.md) by their specific handling of input dimensions, output channels, and internal feature fusion mechanisms.
+Network architectures in NeRF define the function $F_\Theta: (\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{d}) \rightarrow (\sigma, \mathbf{c})$ that maps 3D spatial positions $\mathbf{x}$ and viewing directions $\mathbf{d}$ to volume density $\sigma$ and view-dependent color $\mathbf{c}$ [G]. While the standard NeRF architecture uses two MLPs, variants modify this structure to handle dynamic scenes, noise, specific object manipulations, or efficiency constraints [S1, S3, S5, S6].
 
 ## How it works
